{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785185830,"nonce":"mZaQTZum/nDcQvrgpe7pVqgDJwk=","title":"ADR-0062: Export revocation gives ESTALE, and nfsd restart imposes a 90s grace period plus nfsdcld","message":"ADR-0062 handles a *dead* daemon (hard mounts + the Step deadline + durable retry) but not three measured behaviours around its own lifecycle.\n\n- Revoking an export under a live client gives `ESTALE` then `EACCES`. ADR-0062 says a capability is \"revoked when the Attempt settles\" — that races a Pod still inside SIGTERM grace, which will see stale-handle errors mid-shutdown.\n- Starting `nfsd` logs a **90-second grace period**, during which clients block. That is the restart story part of the ADR's \"drain-then-roll\" leans on, unquantified.\n- It also logs *\"Unable to initialize client recovery tracking! (-110) / Is nfsdcld running?\"* — `nfsdcld` is an undeclared dependency for client recovery across restarts.\n\n\n- [ ] Revocation does not fire while the Attempt's Pod may still be running; the ordering is explicit and tested\n- [ ] A Step that hits `ESTALE` fails with a named cause, never a mystery I/O error (ADR-0027)\n- [ ] The 90s grace window is accounted for in the drain-then-roll upgrade path and in the Step deadline budget\n- [ ] `nfsdcld` is either a declared dependency or its absence is a deliberate, recorded choice\n\n\n- 3a0f2a2 (s5)","files":null}]}